  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1015 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chances of showers and thunderstorms continue \nthrough Sunday. In addition, heat and humidity will persist through \nthis weekend and into early next week.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict SW flow aloft this morning\nbetween and upper trough over the Great Lakes and a subtropical\nridge offshore. There is a shortwave trough over the upper Ohio\nValley embedded within the flow in advance of the upper trough,\nand this feature is triggering convection this morning from \nnorthern WV to SW PA and the MD panhandle. At the surface, high\npressure is centered well offshore, with a lee-side trough to \nthe east of the Blue Ridge. Warm and humid with temperatures \nrising through the lower to mid 80s and dewpoints in the mid \n70s.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave trough is expected to push E across\nthe northern Mid-Atlantic this afternoon and evening. Effective\nbulk shear is progged to reach ~30kt by this aftn, with MLCAPE\npotentially reaching 2000-3000J/kg. Additional showers/tstms are\nexpected to develop in vicinity of the Blue Ridge and the lee\ntrough this aftn when the convective temp is reached. The main \nquestion is how far southward extent of tstms. Given sufficient \nshear and instability, initial cellular convection has the \npotential to organize into line segments. The main threat will \nbe damaging wind gusts with 800-1000J/kg of DCAPE and steep low-\nlevel lapse rates, but some large hail is possible in any \ninitial cellular convection. SPC has expanded the marginal risk \narea southward to basically I-64 from the Louisa/Fluvanna line \nto the north-side of the RIC metro, then eastward to about \nWallops Island. A slight risk has been added immediately N of \nthe local area.\n\nOtherwise, a normal summer time pattern will be in place with \nscattered cumulus clouds in the afternoon and temperatures \nreaching into the low 90s. Dew points will once again struggle \nto mix out through the day and remain in the low to middle 70s. \nThe mix of heat and humidity will allow Heat Indices to be in \nthe upper 90s and low 100s, but will remain shy of the 105F Heat\nAdvisory Criteria. For Sunday, the weather pattern will remain \nquite similar with the chance of showers and thunderstorms along\nthe Lee Trough, high terrain, and Sea Breeze in the afternoon. \nIn addition to the showers and thunderstorms, the heat and \nhumidity will persist with temps ranging in the low to middle \n90s and Heat Indices between the upper 90s and low 100s but \nagain remaining shy of the 105F Heat Advisory criteria. \n\nBy early next week, ensembles continue to hint on a more zonal \npattern across the area with the strongest flow remaining north of \nthe area. While at the surface high pressure continues to thrive off \nthe coast continuing to usher in heat and moisture. During the early \nto mid-week stretch the chances of showers and storms persist with \nthe best chance  now possibly being late Tuesday into early \nWednesday morning as a stronger cold front tries to move into the \narea. Outside of the daily chances of showers and storms, \ntemperatures are expected to rise above normal with temperatures \npossibly reaching into the lower 90s. With dew points struggling to \nmix through the day as the high is offshore they will remain the low \nto middle 70s again allowing Heat Indices to be in the low 100s. \nWill note there is a possibility that some places could reach Heat \nAdvisory criteria Monday through Wednesday but confidence at this \ntime remains low.",
